REV.page({"course":"python","id":"w05","title":"Functions: def, return and the modular approach","summary":"How to make your own commands in Python. A function gives a block of code a name and some inputs; calling it runs the block; return hands a value back so that the rest of the program can use it. With functions, the list programs and the obvious sort of Week 4 become small, tidy pieces: the modular approach.","n":5,"lectures":[{"code":"5.1","title":"Introduction to functions | hands-on coding with examples","video":"zDZRfWWetg0","seconds":932},{"code":"5.2","title":"More examples of functions","video":"TBFTFusLIco","seconds":872},{"code":"5.3","title":"Sorting in python using functions refactoring the obvious sort","video":"8MQBieBCRFA","seconds":680},{"code":"5.4","title":"Conclusion | mastering fundamentals & preparing for challenges","video":"Z-1CxNrmRR4","seconds":162}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"your-own-commands-def","level":2,"title":"Your own commands: def"},{"id":"a-function-for-a-discount","level":2,"title":"A function for a discount"},{"id":"return-a-value-you-can-use","level":2,"title":"return: a value you can use"},{"id":"a-program-that-uses-a-function","level":2,"title":"A program that uses a function"},{"id":"functions-on-lists","level":2,"title":"Functions on lists"},{"id":"a-list-as-the-input","level":3,"title":"A list as the input"},{"id":"minimum-and-maximum","level":3,"title":"Minimum and maximum"},{"id":"joining-two-lists","level":3,"title":"Joining two lists"},{"id":"the-average","level":3,"title":"The average"},{"id":"functions-stay-loaded-the-modular-approach","level":3,"title":"Functions stay loaded: the modular approach"},{"id":"sorting-with-functions","level":2,"title":"Sorting with functions"},{"id":"the-obvious-sort-in-one-function","level":3,"title":"The obvious sort in one function"},{"id":"a-helper-function-min-list","level":3,"title":"A helper function: min_list"},{"id":"the-moral-small-modules","level":3,"title":"The moral: small modules"},{"id":"conclusion-code-and-code-again","level":2,"title":"Conclusion: code, and code again"},{"id":"function-mistakes-and-how-to-spot-them","level":2,"title":"Function mistakes and how to spot them"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>function</strong> is a named block of code. You <strong>define</strong> it once with <code>def name(<wbr>parameters):</code> and an indented body, and you <strong>call</strong> it as often as you like with <code>name(values)</code>. The lecturer: you are “creating your own commands”.</li>\n<li><strong>Defining is not running.</strong> A <code>def</code> only stores the function; the body runs at each call. The values in a call go into the parameters <strong>in order</strong>: in <code>discount(x,<wbr>y)</code>, <code>x</code> becomes <code>cost</code> and <code>y</code> becomes <code>d</code>, whatever their names outside.</li>\n<li><strong><code>return</code> versus <code>print</code>.</strong> <code>print</code> only shows a value on the screen. <code>return</code> hands the value back to the caller, so <code>add(a,b)+10</code> can use it. A function without <code>return</code> gives back <code>None</code>, and <code>None + 10</code> stops the program with <code>TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'NoneType' and 'int'</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Functions on lists</strong> take a list and give back a value (the first entry, the minimum, the maximum, the average) or a new list (two lists joined).</li>\n<li><strong>A function can call another function.</strong> The obvious sort of Week 4 becomes <code>min_list</code> (find the smallest entry) plus <code>obvious_sort1</code> (take the smallest out, again and again): “you seek the help of your friend”.</li>\n<li><strong>The modular approach</strong>: break a big problem into small modules, write each one as a function, test it, then stitch them together. It is “easy on our mind”.</li>\n<li>The week’s last video closes the practice programs (birthday paradox, search, sort, matrices) with one piece of advice: type these programs again and again, without looking.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"your-own-commands-def\">Your own commands: def</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.1, Introduction to functions.</em> The lecture first types into the <strong>IPython console</strong> of Spyder, the right half of the screen. There, <code>In [n]:</code> marks what is typed, and the lines below it are Python’s answer.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You already use functions that come with Python: <code>print(...)</code> shows text, <code>input()</code> reads text, <code>int(...)</code> turns text into a whole number, <code>len(...)</code> counts the entries of a list. Each one has a <strong>name</strong> and takes values in <strong>brackets</strong>. Think of a function as a small machine: values go in, the machine does its job, and sometimes a value comes out. This week you build your own machines. The lecturer says that you are “creating your own commands”, new words that Python then understands <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture starts without any explanation and asks you to guess what happens <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">add</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">a,b</span>):\n    ans=a+b\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(ans)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Python shows <strong>nothing</strong>: no output and no error, although <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> never got values <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. These lines only <strong>define</strong> a new command called <code>add</code>; they do not run it. Then:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [2]: add(1,6)\n7\n\nIn [3]: add(10,72)\n82\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>add(1,6)</code> <strong>calls</strong> the function: Python puts 1 into <code>a</code> and 6 into <code>b</code>, runs the indented lines, and the <code>print</code> shows 7 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. The lecturer’s picture: <code>add</code> is a new word, and its body is a <strong>room</strong> where some calculations take place <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Function definition and function call</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>def name(parameter1, parameter2):\n    body line 1\n    body line 2\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>def</code> (short for <em>define</em>) starts a <strong>function definition</strong>. After it come the function’s <strong>name</strong>, its <strong>parameters</strong> in brackets, and a colon.</li>\n<li>A <strong>parameter</strong> is a variable that receives a value when the function is called: here <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>body</strong> is the indented block under the <code>def</code> line, with the same indenting rule as <code>if</code> and the loops.</li>\n<li>A <strong>function call</strong> is the name followed by values in brackets, like <code>add(1,6)</code>. These values are the <strong>arguments</strong>. The call runs the body, with each parameter holding its argument.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Python reads a <code>def</code> without running its body. The body runs only when the function is called, and again at every call.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture itself says “variables” for <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>. “Parameter” and “argument” are the standard words, and Week 6 uses them (6.9, Types of function arguments).</p>\n<p>A second command, <code>sub</code>, is defined the same way <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">sub</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">a,b</span>):\n    ans=a-b\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(ans)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [5]: sub(10,8)\n2\n\nIn [6]: add(8,9)\n17\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>add</code> still works after <code>sub</code> is defined. You define a command once and keep using it: “the idea here is the comfort with which you can define something, and you can keep using it” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-function-for-a-discount\">A function for a discount</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.1, continued.</em> A shop gives a <strong>discount</strong> of <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span></span></span></span></span> percent: the price goes down by <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span></span></span></span></span> rupees for every 100 rupees. The lecture’s example <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>: a cost of 100 rupees with a 10 percent discount should become 90 rupees.</p>\n<section class=\"box fm\" id=\"f1\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Formula</span><span class=\"box-t\">Discounted price</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8623em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">price</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6984em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6984em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0574em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\nwhere cost is the price before the discount, in rupees, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span></span></span></span></span> is the discount in percent, and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6984em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2251em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8801em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">d</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span> is the amount taken off. Example: for cost 100 and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span>,</span> the amount taken off is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">10</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span> rupees and the price is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">90</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s function <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">discount</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">cost,d</span>):\n    ans=cost-(cost*(d/<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>))\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(ans)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Python works out the innermost brackets first: <code>d/100</code>, then <code><span>cost*(d/100)</span></code>, the amount taken off, and last <code><span>cost-(...)</span></code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s 8 percent discount on 1200 rupees (at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The call <code>discount(<wbr>1200,<wbr>8)</code> puts 1200 into <code>cost</code> and 8 into <code>d</code>.</li>\n<li>Innermost brackets: <code>d/100</code> is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">8</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.08</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Next: <code><span>cost*(d/100)</span></code> is <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.08</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">96.0</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> the amount taken off.</li>\n<li>Last: <code><span>cost-(...)</span></code> is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">96.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1104.0</span></span></span></span>,</span> stored in <code>ans</code>.</li>\n<li><code>print(ans)</code> shows <code>1104.0</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecturer checks it by hand: 8 percent of 1200 is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">96</span></span></span></span>,</span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">96</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1104</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>1104.0</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s three calls <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [9]: discount(100,8)\n92.0\n\nIn [10]: discount(1200,8)\n1104.0\n\nIn [11]: discount(12129834,17.5)\n10007113.05\n</code></pre>\n<p>Every answer has a decimal point: <code>/</code> always gives a <strong>float</strong>, a number with a decimal point (Week 1), and a calculation that includes a float gives a float.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A bracket that is never closed</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer first typed <code>ans=<wbr><span>cost-(cost*(d/</span><wbr>100)</code>, with only one <code>)</code> for two <code>(</code>. The console kept showing <code>...:</code> and asking for more lines, because it was still waiting for the last bracket: “that is why it is not stopping” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>. Count the brackets: every <code>(</code> needs its <code>)</code>. In a program file, Python 3.10 and later report <code>SyntaxError: '(<wbr>' was never closed</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Using the results together</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. The lecturer wants <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span></span></span></span>,</span> plus <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span>,</span> plus the discounted price of 1500 rupees at 7.5 percent, all in one line:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [12]: add(17,5)+sub(100,3)+discount(1500,7.5)\n22\n97\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n\n  File &quot;&lt;ipython-input-12-4b12b93049da&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n    add(17,5)+sub(100,3)+discount(1500,7.5)\n\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'NoneType' and 'NoneType'\n</code></pre>\n<p>A <strong>traceback</strong> is Python’s error report: where the error happened, the line itself, and, on the last line, the kind of error with a message. Read the last line first.</p>\n<p>Why the error? <code>add(17,5)</code> <strong>shows</strong> 22 on the screen, but the call does not <strong>become</strong> 22: these functions only print, and give nothing back to the line that called them <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>. In Python, “nothing” is a special value, <code>None</code>, whose type is <code>NoneType</code>. So Python is asked to work out <code>None + None</code>, which has no meaning. Python works from left to right: <code>add</code> printed 22, <code>sub</code> printed 97, then the first <code>+</code> failed, so <code>discount</code> was never called. The lecture’s question <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>: is there a way to <strong>return</strong> the sum, so that <code>add(17,5)</code> can be used like a number?</p>\n<h2 id=\"return-a-value-you-can-use\">return: a value you can use</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.1, continued.</em> The lecture moves to the <strong>editor</strong>, the left half of Spyder: a whole program is written in a file and run, and each run appears in the console as <code>runfile(...)</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">add</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">a,b</span>):\n    ans=a+b\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> ans\n\na=<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\nb=<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(add(a,b))\n</code></pre>\n<p>This prints <code>17</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>. “Return” in everyday English is what you do with an Amazon package <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>. In programming it means that the function hands a value back to the place where it was called: “it will assign this value to the function” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How Python runs this program (the lecture’s walk-through at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lines 1–3: the <code>def</code> stores the function <code>add</code>. Its body does not run yet.</li>\n<li>Lines 5–6: <code>a</code> gets 2 and <code>b</code> gets 15.</li>\n<li>Line 7: to print, Python needs the value of <code>add(a,b)</code>. It <strong>jumps</strong> to the definition of <code>add</code>, where the parameter <code>a</code> gets 2 and <code>b</code> gets 15.</li>\n<li>The body runs: <code>ans=a+b</code> makes <code>ans</code> equal to 17, and <code>return ans</code> hands 17 back and leaves the function.</li>\n<li>Back on line 7, the call <code>add(a,b)</code> is replaced by 17, and <code>print</code> shows it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>17</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A program normally runs from the top line to the bottom line. A call is different: Python jumps into the function, runs its body, and comes back to the line it left <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">return and None</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>return value</code> ends the function at once and hands <code>value</code> back to the caller. The call is replaced by that value, so it can be printed, stored in a variable, or used in a calculation. A function that ends without a <code>return</code> hands back <code>None</code>, a special value that means “no value”; its type is <code>NoneType</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Now the returned value can be used in a calculation <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">add</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">a,b</span>):\n    ans=a+b\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> ans\n\na=<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\nb=<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>\nans=add(a,b)+<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(ans)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This prints <code>27</code>: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span></span></span></span>,</span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">27</span></span></span></span>.</span> Then the lecture changes <code>return ans</code> back to <code>print(ans)</code> and runs the program again <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">add</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">a,b</span>):\n    ans=a+b\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(ans)\n\na=<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\nb=<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>\nans=add(a,b)+<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(ans)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>17\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n\n  File &quot;/Users/srsiyengar/.spyder-py3/temp.py&quot;, line 7, in &lt;module&gt;\n    ans=add(a,b)+10\n\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'NoneType' and 'int'\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>print</code> inside <code>add</code> shows 17, but the call gives back <code>None</code>, so line 7 asks for <code>None + 10</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. An <strong>operand</strong> is a value that an operator such as <code>+</code> works on, and <code>+</code> cannot work on <code>None</code> and a whole number (<code>int</code>). The lesson: to use a function’s result, <code>return</code> it; printing it is not enough.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In the body</th>\n<th>print</th>\n<th>return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shows the value on the screen</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>only if the caller prints it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The call gives back</td>\n<td><code>None</code></td>\n<td>the value of <code>ans</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Then <code>add(a,b)+10</code> gives</td>\n<td>an error</td>\n<td>27</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body lines below it</td>\n<td>still run</td>\n<td>never run: the function has ended</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Out[n] in the console means “returned”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the IPython console, a value that a call <strong>returns</strong> is shown after <code>Out[n]:</code>, while text that a function <strong>prints</strong> has no <code>Out</code>. Compare <code>add(1,6)</code> above (printed, no <code>Out</code>) with <code>first_element(x)</code> in 5.2, which shows <code>Out[3]: 1</code>. In a program file, a returned value is shown only if you print it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Why not just write <code>a+b</code>?</strong> The lecturer admits that <code>ans=a+b+10</code> prints 27 too, and that a function for adding is like using “a big axe to cut a flower” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>. The example is small on purpose; things will get very complicated. Then functions keep you organised: they are “useful mainly for your mind”, because you think in a <strong>modular</strong> way: you break an idea into modules and solve them slowly <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why the name “function”?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In mathematics, a function takes an input and gives exactly one output: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> gives <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span>.</span> A Python function with <code>return</code> works the same way: <code>add(2,15)</code> always gives 17. A Python function can also do things that a maths function cannot, such as printing or reading input.</p>\n</div></section>\n<a class=\"labcard\" href=\"#/labs/sets/functions?rule=x^2\"><span class=\"labcard-k\">Try it in the Sets and functions lab</span><span class=\"labcard-t\">In mathematics: one output for every input</span><span class=\"labcard-go\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a>\n<h2 id=\"a-program-that-uses-a-function\">A program that uses a function</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.1, continued.</em> The discount function, now with <code>return</code>, and the numbers come from the user <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">discount</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">cost,d</span>):\n    ans=cost-(cost*(d/<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>))\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> ans\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enter the cost price&quot;</span>)\nc=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enter the discount&quot;</span>)\ndisc=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The final discount is: &quot;</span>, discount(c,disc))\n</code></pre>\n<p>The first run (100 and 10) printed nothing after the two inputs, because the last line was not written yet <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>. With the last line, the lecture’s run (the user typed 1000, then 11) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Enter the cost price\n1000\nEnter the discount\n11\nThe final discount is:  890.0\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace of the run with 1000 and 11</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>c=<wbr>int(<wbr>input())</code>: the user types 1000; <code>input()</code> gives the text <code>'1000'</code>, and <code>int(...)</code> turns it into the number 1000.</li>\n<li><code>disc</code> becomes 11 in the same way.</li>\n<li><code>discount(<wbr>c,<wbr>disc)</code>: the first argument goes into the first parameter and the second into the second, so <code>cost</code> is 1000 and <code>d</code> is 11.</li>\n<li><code>d/100</code> is <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">11</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.11</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li><code><span>cost*(d/100)</span></code> is <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.11</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">110.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li><code>ans</code> is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">110.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">890.0</span></span></span></span>,</span> and <code>return ans</code> hands it back.</li>\n<li><code>print</code> puts a space between its two items (Week 3’s <code>sep</code>). The text already ends with a space, so two spaces stand before 890.0.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>The final discount is:  890.0</code>. Check: 11 percent of 1000 is 110, and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">110</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">890</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>The names outside do not matter</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>. The cost is called <code>c</code> outside the function but <code>cost</code> inside. Any names will do; the lecture renames <code>c</code> to <code>x</code> and <code>disc</code> to <code>y</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enter the cost price&quot;</span>)\nx=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enter the discount&quot;</span>)\ny=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The final discount is: &quot;</span>, discount(x,y))\n</code></pre>\n<p>When Python meets the word <code>discount</code>, it looks for its definition, sees the parameters <code>cost</code> and <code>d</code>, and takes <code>x</code> as <code>cost</code> and <code>y</code> as <code>d</code>, in that order <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>. The lecturer’s picture: suppose his nickname is Sudhi but the function calls him Tom. You send in Sudhi; inside the function he is Tom, and all the operations happen on Tom <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Arguments go in by position</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In a call, the <strong>first</strong> argument goes into the <strong>first</strong> parameter, the second argument into the second parameter, and so on. Only the order matters, not the names: if <code>c</code> and <code>x</code> are 1000 and <code>disc</code> and <code>y</code> are 11, then <code>discount(<wbr>c,<wbr>disc)</code>, <code>discount(x,<wbr>y)</code> and <code>discount(<wbr>1000,<wbr>11)</code> all give <code>cost</code> = 1000 and <code>d</code> = 11.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Swapping the arguments</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own example: <code>discount(<wbr>11,<wbr>1000)</code> makes <code>cost</code> = 11 and <code>d</code> = 1000, and returns <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">99.0</span></span></span></span> instead of 890.0. Python cannot know that you meant the other order, so there is no error message. Write the arguments in the order of the <code>def</code> line.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>If this feels confusing, the lecturer says, watch the video a couple of times, even a dozen times, and write your own functions; the whole week is about functions <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"functions-on-lists\">Functions on lists</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.2, More examples of functions.</em> Programs from <a href=\"#/python/w04\">Week 4</a>, such as the minimum of a list, written again as functions: “the point is to do the same program, again, using a new concept” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-list-as-the-input\">A list as the input</h3>\n<p>A <strong>list</strong> (Week 4) holds several values in order, in square brackets: <code><span>x=[1,2,3]</span></code>. Each entry has an <strong>index</strong>, its position counted from 0, so <code>x[0]</code> is 1 and <code>x[1]</code> is 2. The lecture types two tiny functions into the console <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">first_element</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">second_element</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [2]: x=[1,2,3]\n\nIn [3]: first_element(x)\nOut[3]: 1\n\nIn [5]: second_element(x)\nOut[5]: 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>The parameter is called <code>l</code>, the list outside is called <code>x</code>: as with <code>discount</code>, the names do not need to match. A function can take a whole list as its input, and it can also return a list <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"minimum-and-maximum\">Minimum and maximum</h3>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not use built-in names for your variables</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>print</code> are functions that come with Python (<strong>built-in</strong> functions). A variable with one of these names hides the function: our own example, after <code>print=0</code> the line <code>print('hi')</code> stops with <code>TypeError: 'int' object is not callable</code>. So the lecture calls its variables <code>mini</code> and <code>maxi</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The plan, as in Week 4: take the first entry as the smallest so far, walk through the list, and whenever an entry is smaller than the smallest so far, it becomes the new smallest <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#Let us write a few functions on lists.</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">list_min</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    mini=l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (l[i]&lt;mini):\n            mini=l[i]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> mini\n\nl=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,-<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(list_min(l))\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>len(l)</code> is the number of entries (8 here), so <code>range(<wbr>len(l))</code> gives the indexes 0 to 7, and <code>l[i]</code> is the entry at index <code>i</code>. The program prints <code><span>-10</span></code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace of list_min on the lecture’s list</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>mini</code> starts as <code>l[0]</code>, which is 1. In each row, the entry is <code>l[i]</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>i</code></th>\n<th>Entry</th>\n<th>Smaller than <code>mini</code>?</th>\n<th>New <code>mini</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>1 &lt; 1</code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>2 &lt; 1</code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>−10</td>\n<td><code><span>-10</span> &lt; 1</code>: <code>True</code></td>\n<td>−10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>6 &lt; <span>-10</span></code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>−10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>4 &lt; <span>-10</span></code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>−10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>After the loop, <code>return mini</code> hands back −10.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code><span>-10</span></code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The maximum is the same function with <code>&gt;</code> in place of <code>&lt;</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">list_maxi</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    maxi=l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (l[i]&gt;maxi):\n            maxi=l[i]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> maxi\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecturer first named this function <code>list_max</code> in the <code>def</code> line but called <code>list_maxi</code>, and the run stopped after printing <code><span>-10</span></code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n\n  File &quot;/Users/srsiyengar/.spyder-py3/temp.py&quot;, line 19, in &lt;module&gt;\n    print(list_maxi(l))\n\nNameError: name 'list_maxi' is not defined\n</code></pre>\n<p>A <strong>NameError</strong> means that Python does not know the name: a call must use exactly the name of the <code>def</code> line. With the function renamed to <code>list_maxi</code>, the program prints <code><span>-10</span></code> and <code>6</code>. With 100 added at the end, <code>l=<wbr>[1,<wbr>2,<wbr>3,<wbr>4,<wbr>5,<wbr><span>-10,</span><wbr>6,<wbr>4,<wbr>100]</code>, it prints <code><span>-10</span></code> and <code>100</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>.</p>\n<a class=\"labcard\" href=\"#/labs/dice/summary?src=typed&amp;x=1,2,3,4,5,-10,6,4,100\"><span class=\"labcard-k\">Try it in the Dice lab</span><span class=\"labcard-t\">The lecture’s list, sorted: its smallest and largest entries and its mean</span><span class=\"labcard-go\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a>\n<h3 id=\"joining-two-lists\">Joining two lists</h3>\n<p><code>list_appendbefore(l,<wbr>z)</code> puts the entries of <code>z</code> <strong>before</strong> the entries of <code>l</code>. The lecture’s example <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>: with <code>l=<wbr>[1,<wbr>2,<wbr>7,<wbr>8,<wbr>9]</code> and <code><span>z=[7,51,1]</span></code>, the answer should be <code>[7,<wbr>51,<wbr>1,<wbr>1,<wbr>2,<wbr>7,<wbr>8,<wbr>9]</code>. The plan: start a new, empty list; append the entries of <code>z</code>; then append the entries of <code>l</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">list_appendbefore</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l,z</span>):\n    newl=[]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(z)):\n        newl.append(z[i])\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        newl.append(l[i])\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> newl\n</code></pre>\n<p><code><span>newl=[]</span></code> is an <strong>empty list</strong>, and <code>newl.<wbr>append(v)</code> adds <code>v</code> at the end of <code>newl</code> (Week 4).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace with the lecture’s lists l = [1, 2, 7, 8, 9] and z = [7, 51, 1]</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To fit a phone screen, the table writes lists without spaces, as the lecture’s code does.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>i</code></th>\n<th>Appended</th>\n<th><code>newl</code> after</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><code>z[0]</code> = 7</td>\n<td><code>[7]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>z[1]</code> = 51</td>\n<td><code>[7,51]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>z[2]</code> = 1</td>\n<td><code>[7,51,1]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><code>l[0]</code> = 1</td>\n<td><code>[7,51,1,1]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>l[1]</code> = 2</td>\n<td><code>[7,51,1,1,2]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>l[2]</code> = 7</td>\n<td><code>[7,<wbr>51,<wbr>1,<wbr>1,<wbr>2,<wbr>7]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code>l[3]</code> = 8</td>\n<td><code>[7,<wbr>51,<wbr>1,<wbr>1,<wbr>2,<wbr>7,<wbr>8]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>l[4]</code> = 9</td>\n<td><code>[7,<wbr>51,<wbr>1,<wbr>1,<wbr>2,<wbr>7,<wbr>8,<wbr>9]</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>The first loop runs over the 3 indexes of <code>z</code>, the second over the 5 indexes of <code>l</code>: 8 appends in all.</li>\n<li><code>return newl</code> hands back the new list; <code>l</code> and <code>z</code> are not changed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>[7, 51, 1, 1, 2, 7, 8, 9]</code>, as the lecture wanted.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s run, with the lists in the file <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">l=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,-<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(list_min(l))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(list_maxi(l))\nz=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(list_appendbefore(l,z))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>-10\n100\n[10, 20, 30, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, -10, 6, 4, 100]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Python prints a list with a space after each comma. The <code>return newl</code> line was missing at first, and the lecturer added it just before the run: without a <code>return</code> there is no error message, “but it will not do what it is supposed to do” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>. (The call would give back <code>None</code>, and <code>print</code> would show <code>None</code>.)</p>\n<p><strong>Appending at the end</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>. The same function with the two loops swapped:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">list_appendend</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l,z</span>):\n    newl=[]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        newl.append(l[i])\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(z)):\n        newl.append(z[i])\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> newl\n</code></pre>\n<p>With <code>print(<wbr>list_appendend(l,<wbr>z))</code> added, the run prints one more line <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, -10, 6, 4, 100, 10, 20, 30]\n</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"the-average\">The average</h3>\n<p>The <strong>average</strong> (the mean, from Statistics 1) of a list is the sum of its entries divided by the number of entries <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box fm\" id=\"f2\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Formula</span><span class=\"box-t\">Average of a list</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.2222em;\"><span class=\"mord\">ˉ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.9463em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.2603em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">⋯</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\nwhere <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> are the entries of the list, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span></span> is the number of entries (<code>len(l)</code> in Python), and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.5678em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.2222em;\"><span class=\"mord\">ˉ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>,</span> read “x bar”, is the average.</p>\n</div></section>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">list_average</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>+l[i]\n    ans=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>/<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> ans\n\nl=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(list_average(l))\n</code></pre>\n<p>This prints <code>6.5</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>. <code>sum</code> starts at 0 and collects the entries one by one, like the running totals of Week 3.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace for l = [1, 7, 8, 10] (the lecture’s check at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>i</code></th>\n<th><code>l[i]</code></th>\n<th><code>sum=sum+l[i]</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>After the loop, <code>sum</code> is 26 and <code>len(l)</code> is 4.</li>\n<li><code>ans=<wbr>sum/<wbr>len(l)</code> is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">26</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6.5</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>6.5</code>. The lecturer also checks it in the console: <code>1+7+8+10</code> gives <code>Out[14]: 26</code>, and <code>26/4</code> gives <code>Out[15]: 6.5</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">sum is a built-in name too</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>sum</code> is also a built-in Python function. Inside <code>list_average</code>, the name <code>sum</code> is the function’s own variable, so the program works, but inside this function the built-in <code>sum</code> is hidden. By the lecture’s own advice about <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>, a name like <code>total</code> is safer.</p>\n</div></section>\n<a class=\"labcard\" href=\"#/labs/dice/summary?src=typed&amp;x=1,7,8,10\"><span class=\"labcard-k\">Try it in the Dice lab</span><span class=\"labcard-t\">The mean of 1, 7, 8 and 10, worked out step by step</span><span class=\"labcard-go\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a>\n<h3 id=\"functions-stay-loaded-the-modular-approach\">Functions stay loaded: the modular approach</h3>\n<p>Once the file has run, its functions “sit in your memory”, and you can call them from the console with any list <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>In [17]: list_maxi([1,-1,10,6,-2])\nOut[17]: 10\n</code></pre>\n<p>“You have now authored a function” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>. The lecturer calls this the <strong>modular approach to programming</strong>: very patiently, you break your problem into smaller pieces, write the pieces as functions, and finally stitch them together into the final program <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>. His sketch, described but not coded <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>: ask the user for a list, ask what to find (the average, the minimum or the maximum), and call the matching function. The main program stays short, because the work sits inside the functions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sorting-with-functions\">Sorting with functions</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.3, Sorting using functions: refactoring the obvious sort.</em> To <strong>refactor</strong> a program is to rewrite it in a better-organised way without changing what it does. The lecture refactors the <strong>obvious sort</strong> of Week 4 (lecture 4.6) to show “how both time and effort gets reduced if you use functions” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The obvious way to sort a list from smallest to largest <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>: find the smallest entry, move it to a new list, and repeat with what is left. The new list fills up in order: first the smallest entry, then the next smallest, and so on.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-obvious-sort-in-one-function\">The obvious sort in one function</h3>\n<p>The lecturer codes it slowly, stumbling on purpose, thinking aloud as a beginner would <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. The three comment lines are the plan (the misspelling “mimimum” is on the screen):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#find out the mimimum most element in the list l</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#append that to a new list x.</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#remove the minimum from the original list l.</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">obvious_sort</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n\n    x=[]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">while</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)&gt;<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>):\n        mini=l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]\n        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n            <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> l[i]&lt;mini :\n                mini=l[i]\n        x.append(mini)\n        l.remove(mini)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> l\n\nl=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">90</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">23</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">97</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">88</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(obvious_sort(l))\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code><span>x=[]</span></code> is the new list that collects the sorted entries.</li>\n<li>The <code>for</code> loop is the minimum-finding loop of <code>list_min</code>.</li>\n<li><code>x.<wbr>append(<wbr>mini)</code> adds the minimum at the end of <code>x</code>, and <code>l.<wbr>remove(<wbr>mini)</code> deletes that value from <code>l</code>, where it first appears (Week 4).</li>\n<li>All this must repeat until <code>l</code> is empty, so it sits inside <code>while(<wbr>len(l)&gt;0):</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>. Every pass removes one entry, so <code>l</code> gets shorter and the loop is sure to end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The run printed <code>[]</code>, an empty list <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>. The mistake was <code>return l</code>: by the end, every entry has been removed from <code>l</code>. The sorted list is <code>x</code>. With <code>return x</code>, the program prints <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>[1, 5, 23, 88, 90, 97]\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace on the lecture’s list [90, 23, 97, 88, 5, 1]</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Before the first pass, <code>l</code> is <code>[90,<wbr>23,<wbr>97,<wbr>88,<wbr>5,<wbr>1]</code> and <code>x</code> is <code>[]</code>. Each row is one pass of the <code>while</code> loop: the smallest entry that the <code>for</code> loop finds (<code>mini</code>), then the two lists after <code>append</code> and <code>remove</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Smallest</th>\n<th><code>l</code> after</th>\n<th><code>x</code> after</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>[90, 23, 97, 88, 5]</td>\n<td>[1]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>[90, 23, 97, 88]</td>\n<td>[1, 5]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>[90, 97, 88]</td>\n<td>[1, 5, 23]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>88</td>\n<td>[90, 97]</td>\n<td>[1, 5, 23, 88]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>90</td>\n<td>[97]</td>\n<td>[1, 5, 23, 88, 90]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>97</td>\n<td>[]</td>\n<td>[1, 5, 23, 88, 90, 97]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>In each pass, the <code>for</code> loop finds the smallest entry of <code>l</code>, <code>x.<wbr>append(<wbr>mini)</code> adds it to the end of <code>x</code>, and <code>l.<wbr>remove(<wbr>mini)</code> deletes it from <code>l</code>.</li>\n<li>After the 6th pass, <code>l</code> is <code>[]</code>, so <code>len(l)&gt;0</code> is <code>False</code> and the loop ends.</li>\n<li>A list of 6 entries needs 6 passes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>return x</code> gives <code>[1, 5, 23, 88, 90, 97]</code>; <code>return l</code> gives <code>[]</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The sort empties your list</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own check: after <code>print(<wbr>obvious_sort(l))</code>, the list <code>l</code> outside the function is <code>[]</code> too. The function receives the list itself, not a copy, so <code>l.<wbr>remove(...)</code> inside the function removes the entries from the caller’s list. Sort the same list twice and the second call returns <code>[]</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-helper-function-min-list\">A helper function: min_list</h3>\n<p>Finding the smallest entry is a job of its own, so it gets a function of its own <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. The file now reads as follows (the three plan comments at the top are left out here):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#find out the mimimum most element in the list l</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">min_list</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    mini=l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)):\n        <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> (l[i]&lt;mini):\n            mini=l[i]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> mini\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">obvious_sort1</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">l</span>):\n    x=[]\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">while</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(l)&gt;<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>):\n        mini=min_list(l)\n        x.append(mini)\n        l.remove(mini)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> x\n\nl=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">90</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">23</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">97</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">88</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(obvious_sort1(l))\n</code></pre>\n<p>The old <code>obvious_sort</code> is still in the file, scrolled out of sight (“let us not see it”), and is no longer called. <code>mini=<wbr>min_list(l)</code> “is the climax”: one line now does the work of the whole <code>for</code> loop, because <code>min_list</code> puts the smallest value of <code>l</code> into <code>mini</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>. The new sort reads like its plan: find the minimum, append it to <code>x</code>, remove it from <code>l</code>, while <code>l</code> is not empty. The first version “was a little heavy on the mind”; this one is not <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Helper function</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>helper function</strong> is a function that another function calls to do one part of its job. When <code>obvious_sort1</code> reaches <code>min_list(l)</code>, Python jumps into <code>min_list</code>, runs it, and comes back with the minimum. The lecturer: “you seek the help of your friend” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The same slip happened again: the first run had <code>return l</code> and printed <code>[]</code>; with <code>return x</code> it printed <code>[1, 5, 23, 88, 90, 97]</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">One function’s output, another’s input</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>mini=<wbr>min_list(l)</code> uses the result of one function inside another, and <code>print(<wbr>add(a,<wbr>b))</code> in 5.1 does the same. In mathematics this is <strong>composition</strong>: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">g</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">))</span></span></span></span></span> means “first apply <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span></span></span></span>,</span> then apply <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">g</span></span></span></span></span> to the result”. See <a href=\"#/labs/functions/composition\" class=\"lablink\">the composition lab</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-moral-small-modules\">The moral: small modules</h3>\n<p>“A big program can be made into bits and pieces and you can solve them slowly” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. Life works the same way: a big task that you keep putting off becomes easy when you break it into small chunks and finish them one by one. The lecture writes the lesson into the file <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#We just learnt that breaking our problem</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#into smaller modules and solving them</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#makes it easy on our mind.</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecturer calls this the <strong>functional approach</strong> to programming, also called the <strong>modular approach</strong>, and the most important idea so far. Practise by rewriting all the code you have seen using functions <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8MQBieBCRFA&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>. The next video uses functions for matrix multiplication, which was “very tough on our mind” without them (lecture 6.4 in <a href=\"#/python/w06\">Week 6</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion-code-and-code-again\">Conclusion: code, and code again</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 5.4, Conclusion.</em> The slides of this short video are titled “Week 4: Practice Programs”, and list the birthday paradox, search, sort and matrix operations: it closes the practice programs, which are revisited “in the forthcoming week”. This week’s 5.3 and Week 6’s 6.4 do that with functions. The advice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Watching is not enough; sit and code. Type these programs without looking at the video or any other reference, so that the basic syntax comes without looking it up <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-1CxNrmRR4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>.</li>\n<li>These programs will come back, so get familiar with them. Post questions on the forum <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-1CxNrmRR4&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>.</li>\n<li>This was the foundation. Next come “bricks” on top of it: some easy, some medium, some hard, some very hard <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-1CxNrmRR4&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</li>\n<li>The pro tip: code the same program multiple times <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-1CxNrmRR4&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</li>\n<li>For fast learners, the instructors post three or four optional challenge questions. If you understood all the code of the week, “that should be more than enough” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-1CxNrmRR4&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>.</li>\n<li>As you learn more Python, programming gets easier and less complex <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-1CxNrmRR4&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"function-mistakes-and-how-to-spot-them\">Function mistakes and how to spot them</h2>\n<p>The messages shown in the lectures come from Python 3.8 (5.1, 5.2) and 3.9 (5.3); newer versions word some of them differently, as noted. The examples marked “our own” are not from the lectures.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">print instead of return</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If a function only prints its result, the call gives back <code>None</code>. Using it in a calculation stops the program:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>ans=<wbr>add(a,<wbr>b)+10</code> gives <code>TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'NoneType' and 'int'</code> (5.1, at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>);</li>\n<li><code>add(<wbr>17,<wbr>5)+sub(<wbr>100,<wbr>3)</code> gives <code>... for +: 'NoneType' and 'NoneType'</code> (5.1, at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The sign: <code>NoneType</code> in the message. The fix: <code>return</code> the value.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Returning the wrong value, or none at all</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>These give no error message, only a wrong answer. In 5.3, <code>return l</code> instead of <code>return x</code> printed <code>[]</code>, twice. A function with its <code>return</code> line forgotten gives <code>None</code>, and printing the call shows <code>None</code>. Check the last line of every function: does it return the variable that holds the answer?</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A name that does not match: NameError</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>def list_max(l):</code> with the call <code>list_maxi(l)</code> gave <code>NameError: name 'list_maxi' is not defined</code> (5.2). Python 3.10 and later add a hint: <code>Did you mean: 'list_max'?</code> Our own example: a call written <strong>above</strong> its <code>def</code> in a file gives the same kind of error, <code>NameError: name 'add' is not defined</code>, because Python runs a file from the top, and at that line <code>add</code> is not defined yet.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A def with no body</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every <code>def</code> needs at least one indented line under it. In 5.1 a lone <code>def discount(<wbr>cost,<wbr>d):</code> was run in the console <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zDZRfWWetg0&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>  File &quot;&lt;ipython-input-7-9dfb8adc63d4&gt;&quot;, line 1\n    def discount(cost,d):\n                         ^\nSyntaxError: unexpected EOF while parsing\n</code></pre>\n<p>EOF means “end of file”: the input ended before the function had a body. In 5.2 the file ran while <code>def list_appendend(l,<wbr>z):</code> had no body yet, and Python reported <code>IndentationError: expected an indented block</code> at the next line, <code>l=<wbr>[1,<wbr>2,<wbr>3,<wbr>4,<wbr>5,<wbr><span>-10,</span><wbr>6,<wbr>4,<wbr>100]</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>. Python 3.10 and later say <code>IndentationError: expected an indented block after function definition on line ...</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The wrong number of arguments</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own examples, with <code>add(a,b)</code> from 5.1: <code>add(5)</code> gives <code>TypeError: add() missing 1 required positional argument: 'b'</code>, and <code>add(1,2,3)</code> gives <code>TypeError: add() takes 2 positional arguments but 3 were given</code>. A call needs one argument for each parameter.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">An empty list</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own examples: <code>list_min([])</code> stops at <code>mini=l[0]</code> with <code>IndexError: list index out of range</code>, because an empty list has no index 0. <code>list_average([])</code> stops at <code>sum/len(l)</code> with <code>ZeroDivisionError: division by zero</code>, because <code>len([])</code> is 0. The lecture’s functions assume a list with at least one entry.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">ans inside and ans outside</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In 5.1, <code>ans</code> appears inside <code>add</code> and also outside it, in <code>ans=<wbr>add(a,<wbr>b)+10</code>. They are two different variables: the parameters, and the variables made inside a function, belong to that function alone. Our own example: run <code>add(2,3)</code> with no <code>ans</code> outside, then <code>print(ans)</code> outside the function gives <code>NameError: name 'ans' is not defined</code>. Week 6 explains this in 6.12, Scope of variables.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Test each function as soon as you write it</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer runs every new function on a small list whose answer he already knows: −10 for the minimum, 100 after adding 100, 6.5 for the average, checked in the console with <code>1+7+8+10</code> and <code>26/4</code>. As he says, programmers write the code and then check it, because the run shows whether it was right or wrong <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TBFTFusLIco&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Define</strong>: <code>def name(<wbr>p1, p2):</code> and an indented body. A <code>def</code> runs nothing; it stores the function.</li>\n<li><strong>Call</strong>: <code>name(a1, a2)</code> runs the body with <code>p1</code> = <code>a1</code> and <code>p2</code> = <code>a2</code>: by position, whatever the names outside.</li>\n<li><strong>return</strong>: <code>return value</code> ends the function and the call is replaced by <code>value</code>. With no <code>return</code>, the call gives <code>None</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>print vs return</strong>: <code>print</code> shows a value; <code>return</code> hands it back. To use a result in a calculation, return it: <code>None + 10</code> is a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Discount</strong>: <code><span>cost-(cost*(d/</span><wbr>100))</code>, that is <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6984em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6984em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2251em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8801em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">d</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span>;</span></span> <code>/</code> gives a float, so <code>discount(<wbr>1200,<wbr>8)</code> is 1104.0.</li>\n<li><strong>Lists</strong>: <code>l[0]</code> is the first entry; minimum and maximum: start from <code>l[0]</code> and update with <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&gt;</code>; join two lists with two <code>append</code> loops; average = <code>sum/len(l)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Names</strong>: not <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>print</code> (or <code>sum</code>) for variables; the call must use the exact name of the <code>def</code> line.</li>\n<li><strong>Helper functions</strong>: <code>obvious_sort1</code> calls <code>min_list</code>. The obvious sort removes the minimum from <code>l</code> and appends it to <code>x</code> until <code>l</code> is empty, so <code>return x</code>, not <code>l</code>; the caller’s list ends up empty.</li>\n<li><strong>Modular approach</strong>: break a big problem into small functions, test each one, then stitch them together.</li>\n<li><strong>Errors</strong>: <code>NameError</code> (unknown name), <code>IndentationError</code> (a <code>def</code> with no body), <code>SyntaxError</code> (a bracket left open), <code>TypeError</code> (<code>None</code> in a sum, or the wrong number of arguments).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">With the first <code>add</code> of 5.1 (the one with <code>print(ans)</code> in its body), what does <code>x=add(2,3)</code> followed by <code>print(x)</code> print, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>add(2,3)</code> runs the body with <code>a</code> = 2 and <code>b</code> = 3: <code>ans</code> is 5, and <code>print(ans)</code> shows <code>5</code>.</li>\n<li>The body ends without a <code>return</code>, so the call gives back <code>None</code>, and <code>x</code> is <code>None</code>.</li>\n<li><code>print(x)</code> shows <code>None</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> two lines, <code>5</code> and then <code>None</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In 5.1, the line that adds <code>add(17,5)</code>, <code>sub(100,3)</code> and <code>discount(<wbr>1500,<wbr>7.5)</code> printed 22 and 97, then stopped. Why is the discount never printed? What does the line give when all three functions use <code>return</code>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>With <code>print</code> in the bodies:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Python works from left to right. <code>add(17,5)</code> prints 22 and gives back <code>None</code>; <code>sub(100,3)</code> prints 97 and gives back <code>None</code>.</li>\n<li>The first <code>+</code> must work out <code>None + None</code>. That is impossible, so the program stops with the <code>TypeError</code> before <code>discount</code> is ever called.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With <code>return</code> in the bodies:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>add(17,5)</code> is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">22</span></span></span></span></span> and <code>sub(100,3)</code> is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">97</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><code>discount(<wbr>1500,<wbr>7.5)</code>: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">7.5</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.075</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> then <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1500</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.075</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">112.5</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> then <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1500</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">112.5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1387.5</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">22</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">97</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">119</span></span></span></span>,</span> and <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">119</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1387.5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1506.5</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> with <code>print</code>, the error comes before <code>discount</code> runs; with <code>return</code>, the line gives <code>1506.5</code> (and prints nothing on its own).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">With the <code>return</code> version of <code>discount</code>, what do <code>discount(<wbr>200,<wbr>25)</code> and <code>discount(<wbr>25,<wbr>200)</code> give?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>For <code>discount(<wbr>200,<wbr>25)</code>: <code>cost</code> = 200 and <code>d</code> = 25.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">25</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.25</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">150.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p>For <code>discount(<wbr>25,<wbr>200)</code>: <code>cost</code> = 25 and <code>d</code> = 200.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">200</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">25.0</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>150.0</code> and <code><span>-25.0</span></code>. The arguments go in by position, so swapping them changes the answer, and there is no error message.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What do <code>list_appendbefore(<wbr>[1,<wbr>2],<wbr>[3])</code> and <code>list_appendend(<wbr>[1,<wbr>2],<wbr>[3])</code> return?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Here <code>l</code> is <code>[1,2]</code> and <code>z</code> is <code>[3]</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>list_appendbefore</code> appends the entries of <code>z</code> first: <code>newl</code> becomes <code>[3]</code>, then <code>[3, 1]</code>, then <code>[3, 1, 2]</code>.</li>\n<li><code>list_appendend</code> appends the entries of <code>l</code> first: <code>newl</code> becomes <code>[1]</code>, then <code>[1, 2]</code>, then <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>[3, 1, 2]</code> and <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p>Here is a small program that uses <code>min_list</code> and <code>obvious_sort1</code> from 5.3:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">l=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(obvious_sort1(l))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(obvious_sort1(l))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(l)\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does this program with obvious_sort1 print, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>First call: the passes take out 1, then 2, then 3, so <code>x</code> is <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> and is returned. Each <code>l.<wbr>remove(<wbr>mini)</code> removed an entry from the list <code>l</code> itself, so <code>l</code> is now <code>[]</code>.</li>\n<li>Second call: <code>len(l)&gt;0</code> is <code>False</code> at once, the loop never runs, and the empty <code>x</code> is returned.</li>\n<li><code>print(l)</code>: the list was emptied by the first call.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>, then <code>[]</code>, then <code>[]</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Trace <code>list_maxi(<wbr>[1,<wbr><span>-1,</span><wbr>10,<wbr>6,<wbr><span>-2])</span></code>, the lecture’s console call in 5.2. What does it return?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><code>maxi</code> starts as <code>l[0]</code>, which is 1. In each row, the entry is <code>l[i]</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>i</code></th>\n<th>Entry</th>\n<th>Bigger than <code>maxi</code>?</th>\n<th>New <code>maxi</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>1 &gt; 1</code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td><code><span>-1</span> &gt; 1</code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><code>10 &gt; 1</code>: <code>True</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>6 &gt; 10</code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td><code><span>-2</span> &gt; 10</code>: <code>False</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>10</code>, as in the lecture’s <code>Out[17]: 10</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
